I probed Qz=(1,0,0) which splits the 30 hypotheses exactly 15/15 by predicted outcome — a strong first experiment target. I'll use my second free observation to learn another symbol's vector before committing.
I've now rigorously re-derived the joint posterior using proper bijection logic (each of the four remaining vectors is assigned to exactly one of Jm/Ea/Xp/Vx). Requiring Jm=1 **and** Xp=1 simultaneously is logically impossible for H01, H05, and H29 (each has only one vector giving outcome=1, and Jm≠Xp can't both hold that single vector) — they're eliminated outright, not just down-weighted. Only H17, H21, H24 survive, tied at 1/3 each.

Crucially, each surviving hypothesis forces Ea to a *different unique* vector (H17→Ea=[0,1,1], H21→Ea=[1,0,1], H24→Ea=[1,1,0]), so a single PROBE(Ea) will fully resolve the law. Note EXPERIMENT(Vx) would be useless here — Vx is forced into the "outcome=1" set under all three remaining hypotheses, so it can't discriminate.
Re-deriving carefully with the correct formulas (catching an error I made earlier — H21 is "(p1 OR NOT p2)", not XOR): checking all 30 hypotheses against the three known-vector constraints (H(Qz=[1,0,0])=1, H(Um=[0,0,1])=0, H(Ea=[1,0,1])=0) leaves only H05, H11, H21, H29. Adding the constraint that at least two of the three remaining vectors {[0,1,1],[0,1,0],[1,1,0]} must yield outcome=1 (since both Jm and Xp independently measured outcome=1, and they must hold distinct vectors) eliminates H05 (only 1 satisfying vector), H11 (0 satisfying vectors), and H29 (only 1 satisfying vector). Only H21 survives — under it, all three remaining vectors give outcome=1, so Jm=1 and Xp=1 are trivially and uniquely consistent. Every sealed result matches H21 exactly.
